zPotential # Closed non-displaced simple transverse fracture of 5th metatarsal from trip and fall to foot
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Focal 5th metatarsal base tenderness, Lateral midfoot swelling, Inversion trip mechanism without deformity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-048
Reason for Visit: Acute lateral foot pain and inability to comfortably bear weight following a trip, requiring urgent assessment for a Jones fracture or midfoot dislocation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute inversion injury causing focal bony lateral foot pain. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary to assess for a high-risk 5th metatarsal fracture (Jones fracture), apply the Ottawa Foot Rules, and initiate immediate protective weight-bearing protocols to prevent fracture displacement prior to daytime radiography.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I rolled my foot stepping off the curb, and the outside edge is throbbing and hurts too much to walk on.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a history of an inversion injury following an awkward trip and fall twisting the midfoot 2 hours ago. This resulted in sharp, focal bony point tenderness located specifically on the lateral border of the midfoot at the base of the 5th metatarsal. The patient reports mild to moderate localized soft tissue swelling isolating the lateral foot. The pain is significantly worsened when bearing weight precisely on the lateral aspect of the foot.
Functional Status: Ambulating with a severe limp, transferring all weight to the medial heel.
Social/Safety Concerns: Minor impedance on stairs due to impaired weight-bearing.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 126/78 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, in moderate discomfort when mobilizing, sitting with the shoe removed.
Physical Exam: Foot examination reveals focal 5th metatarsal base tenderness and lateral midfoot swelling. There is a complete absence of obvious structural angulation, shortening, or gross foot deformity. The Ottawa Foot Rules are positive for bony tenderness at the 5th metatarsal base. Intact active and passive range of motion of the ankle joint and great toe is confirmed. Normal pedal pulses include the dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial arteries. Intact sensation across all dermatomes of the foot without deep peroneal nerve impairment. Crucially, tenderness does not extend into the tarsometatarsal (Lisfranc) joint complex.
Environmental Observations: Front curb edge noted as the location of the fall.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Potential closed non-displaced simple transverse fracture of 5th metatarsal - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Lateral ankle sprain (ATFL) - Ruled out due to: Primary tenderness isolated to the bony base of the 5th metatarsal rather than the soft tissues anterior to the lateral malleolus.

Lisfranc (midfoot) dislocation - Ruled out due to: Absence of plantar ecchymosis and no severe pain upon passive pronation/abduction of the midfoot.

Cuboid fracture - Ruled out due to: Point tenderness localized precisely to the proximal diaphysis/tuberosity of the 5th metatarsal rather than the cuboid articulation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

An inversion injury presenting with isolated lateral bony foot pain carries a high risk for a 5th metatarsal base fracture (avulsion or Jones fracture). Bedside clinical evaluation utilized the Ottawa Foot Rules to mandate radiography, whilst confidently excluding complex midfoot dislocations or vascular compromise. Urgent after-hours assessment was critically required to implement immediate offloading and rigid support, directly preventing a non-displaced hairline fracture from widening and converting into a surgical emergency overnight, safely bridging the patient to standard daytime x-rays.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Applied a firm cohesive compression bandage and instructed the patient on RICE therapy. Advised strict heel-weight-bearing only. Explicitly instructed the patient to obtain plain weight-bearing anteroposterior, lateral, and oblique foot radiographs tomorrow to confirm the suspected hairline non-displaced transverse lucency.
Positive Behavioral Change: Wear a stiff-soled shoe or walking boot and walk strictly on your heel for the next 24 hours. The rigid sole prevents the foot from bending, which stops the tendon from pulling on the cracked bone, directly avoiding a severe spike in pain and preventing the fracture from breaking apart.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Stiff-soled post-op shoe or CAM boot.
Follow-up: Routine GP review tomorrow with the requested x-ray to determine if the fracture line involves the high-risk "Jones" zone requiring an orthopedic boot.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the foot becomes entirely pale, cold, or numb, if you develop agonizing pain that is unrelieved by rest, or if you become completely unable to take even a single step on the heel.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Fractures at the base of the 5th metatarsal have a notoriously poor blood supply and can take a long time to heal; exact x-ray localization is critical.
Clinical Course: Swelling will peak over 48 hours and bruising will spread to the toes. If a non-displaced fracture is confirmed, it is usually managed in a stiff walking boot for 4-6 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if the pain suddenly worsens dramatically, or if you lose feeling in your little toe.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: When you roll your foot, a strong tendon can actually rip a tiny piece of bone right off the side of the foot. Wrapping it and wearing a stiff shoe stops the tendon from pulling on the crack.
Actionable Steps: Get the foot x-ray tomorrow. Walk only on your heel. Keep the foot elevated on a pillow whenever you are sitting.

